1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,733
On the seventh of April, Django

2
00:00:02,767 --> 00:00:06,000
four-point-two reached end of life.

3
00:00:06,033 --> 00:00:08,367
No more security fixes.

4
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:11,117
But Debian thirteen and Ubuntu

5
00:00:11,150 --> 00:00:13,583
twenty-four-oh-four still ship it.

6
00:00:13,617 --> 00:00:15,983
Since then, Django has published nineteen

7
00:00:16,017 --> 00:00:18,433
security advisories — and not one official

8
00:00:18,467 --> 00:00:20,367
patch for four-point-two.

9
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:23,567
On October first, Ubuntu shipped a

10
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:25,717
backport for one of them.

11
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:28,367
Five days later, Django said its original

12
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:30,117
fix was incomplete.

13
00:00:30,150 --> 00:00:32,583
We took Ubuntu's source package, ran a

14
00:00:32,617 --> 00:00:34,850
test calibrated on Django's own fix

15
00:00:34,883 --> 00:00:37,450
commits… and the code path Django closed

16
00:00:37,483 --> 00:00:40,367
is still reachable in Ubuntu's build.

17
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:44,133
That test was run by Heirloom — an agent

18
00:00:44,167 --> 00:00:47,167
that keeps end-of-life software patched,

19
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:50,050
and proves which old versions are actually

20
00:00:50,083 --> 00:00:51,367
vulnerable.

21
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:55,250
Its first rule: never trust a test that

22
00:00:55,283 --> 00:00:58,350
hasn't failed. For each advisory, Heirloom

23
00:00:58,383 --> 00:01:01,217
writes its own test, proves it fails on

24
00:01:01,250 --> 00:01:04,317
Django's vulnerable code and passes on the

25
00:01:04,350 --> 00:01:07,717
fix… and only then asks the real question:

26
00:01:07,750 --> 00:01:10,367
is the old version affected?

27
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:14,200
Then we let it run the whole campaign:

28
00:01:14,233 --> 00:01:17,033
nineteen advisories, replayed in the order

29
00:01:17,067 --> 00:01:19,067
Django published them.

30
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:21,700
Thirteen candidate backports — each one

31
00:01:21,733 --> 00:01:25,067
failing before the fix, and passing after.

32
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:27,567
Where it couldn't prove something, it

33
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:30,733
filed the evidence instead of guessing.

34
00:01:30,767 --> 00:01:33,233
And when a new fix amended one it had

35
00:01:33,267 --> 00:01:35,533
already backported, it stacked the

36
00:01:35,567 --> 00:01:38,367
follow-up on its own pull request.

37
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:42,433
Across the whole campaign, it asked me one

38
00:01:42,467 --> 00:01:45,217
question — a real judgement call about

39
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:47,833
changing Django's behaviour.

40
00:01:47,867 --> 00:01:50,917
I answered once. It sealed the answer as a

41
00:01:50,950 --> 00:01:53,683
precedent, and the next case reused it

42
00:01:53,717 --> 00:01:55,367
without asking.

43
00:01:56,400 --> 00:01:59,517
We tested the method on cases with known

44
00:01:59,550 --> 00:02:02,567
answers. A naive pipeline once called a

45
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:05,083
vulnerable version safe.

46
00:02:05,117 --> 00:02:07,367
Heirloom: zero times.

47
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:10,667
Every claim in every pull request links to

48
00:02:10,700 --> 00:02:13,733
its neat-logs trace, and every backport

49
00:02:13,767 --> 00:02:17,033
links the Entire session of the agent that

50
00:02:17,067 --> 00:02:18,367
wrote it.

51
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:21,867
Each advisory cost about twenty cents of

52
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:24,300
model time. We modelled the business in

53
00:02:24,333 --> 00:02:26,750
C-F-O dot A-I: what that's worth to the

54
00:02:26,783 --> 00:02:29,367
teams who keep old software alive.

55
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:35,383
Heirloom. Old software, kept honest.

56
00:02:35,417 --> 00:02:40,967
Every verdict is a test run you can open.
